(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-glaucoma-diagnostics-quiz"]={"title":"Glaucoma & Diagnostic Agents Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which classes INCREASE aqueous outflow?","choices":["Beta blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and prostaglandins","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and cholinergic agonists only","Beta blockers alone","Prostaglandins, alpha adrenergic agonists and cholinergic agonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists appear on both sides — they decrease production and increase outflow — which is why they are easy to misplace. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which glaucoma causes blindness?","choices":["Raised pressure causes corneal oedema and then scarring","Raised pressure causes cataract formation","Optic neuropathy, axon loss, field loss, blindness","Raised pressure detaches the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The irreversibility is the point — the axons do not come back, which is why the whole of therapy is aimed at preventing progression rather than recovering vision. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which cosmetic adverse effects should a patient starting a prostaglandin analogue be warned about?","choices":["Permanent pupillary constriction and myopia","Bitter taste and stinging on instillation","Bradycardia and wheeze","Eyelash and iris colour change, with hyperaemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The eyelash effect is marketed as a product in its own right, and iris darkening can be permanent — which is why it is a consent conversation rather than a footnote, particularly when only one eye is treated. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which class is first line for open angle glaucoma, and how often is it given?","choices":["Beta blockers, twice daily","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, three times daily","Cholinergic agonists, four times daily","Prostaglandin analogues, once daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are the most commonly used glaucoma medications and the first line agents. Once daily is the whole dose — and more often is actively worse, not merely unnecessary. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 66"},{"q":"A patient has an intraocular pressure of 26 mmHg with a normal optic nerve and no visual field loss. What is this called?","choices":["Open angle glaucoma","Angle closure glaucoma","Ocular hypertension","Normal tension glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pressure above the normal 10 to 21 mmHg but with no optic nerve damage and no field loss. It is a risk factor rather than a disease, and whether to treat depends on the other risk factors. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A patient with asthma needs a topical beta blocker for glaucoma. Which agent is safer, and why?","choices":["Timolol, because it is beta-1 selective","Levobunolol, because it is more efficacious","Betaxolol, because it is beta-1 selective","Carteolol, because it has no systemic absorption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation, so blocking them raises airway resistance. Betaxolol is the beta-1 selective option on this list; carteolol, timolol and levobunolol are non-selective. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 67-68"},{"q":"What does fluorescein show, and what is it used for?","choices":["Epithelial defects of cornea and conjunctiva","Raised intraocular pressure, for glaucoma screening","Fungal elements, for suspected fungal keratitis","Retinal detachment, by staining the posterior segment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It reveals where the epithelium is broken. Its adverse effects are hypersensitivity and a burning sensation, which is true of most things put on an injured eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why might glaucoma therapy be started in one eye only?","choices":["Because bilateral treatment doubles the systemic absorption to unsafe levels","To judge efficacy and tolerability against the untreated eye","Because only the worse eye requires treatment","To reduce the cost of therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The other eye acts as its own control, which tells you whether the drug is doing anything and whether the patient can tolerate it before both eyes are committed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which systemic adverse effects can a topical ophthalmic beta blocker produce?","choices":["Bradycardia, heart block and bronchoconstriction","Hypertension and tachycardia","Central nervous system depression and apnoea","Bitter taste and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A drop is not a local-only treatment — nasolacrimal absorption avoids first-pass metabolism, so a topical beta blocker can do everything a systemic one does. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A patient has had proparacaine for foreign body removal. What must they be told, and what must you NOT do?","choices":["The eye stays numb for several hours, and a short prescription is reasonable for pain","Numb for 10 to 20 minutes, no blink reflex, never prescribe","Vision will blur for about twenty minutes, and they should not drive","They should keep the eye open to let the drug disperse"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No blink reflex means the eye is unprotected for the duration. And these are explicitly not to be prescribed for home use — repeated application is toxic to the epithelium and delays healing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Besides fundoscopy, what therapeutic use do cycloplegics have?","choices":["Uveitis, to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm","Glaucoma, to increase aqueous outflow","Bacterial conjunctivitis, to reduce photophobia","Dry eye, to reduce tear evaporation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the pupil moving stops the iris adhering, and relaxing the ciliary muscle relieves the pain of spasm. So they are not purely diagnostic agents. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which adverse effects most affect whether a patient will keep taking a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":["Bitter taste and stinging","Bradycardia and heart block","Iris darkening and eyelash growth","Fixed small pupils and myopia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neither is dangerous, but between them they affect over half of patients — and a drop that tastes foul and stings is a drop that gets skipped. The taste comes from nasolacrimal drainage to the pharynx. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does pilocarpine lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["It blocks muscarinic receptors, dilating the pupil","It activates muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","It reduces aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase","It analogues prostaglandin F2-alpha"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contracting the ciliary muscle pulls on the trabecular meshwork and opens the drainage route — an outflow drug achieved by a muscle action. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Phenylephrine also dilates the pupil. How does it differ from the antimuscarinics?","choices":["It is an antimuscarinic with a longer duration","It produces cycloplegia without mydriasis","It constricts rather than dilates","It is a sympathomimetic, more light-reactive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two routes to mydriasis — block the constrictor or stimulate the dilator — and the sympathomimetic leaves more light reactivity, which matters if the pupil response still needs assessing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why are younger patients usually intolerant of miotic therapy?","choices":["Systemic bradycardia from muscarinic activation","Visual blurring from induced myopia","Permanent iris colour change","Increased airway resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fixed small pupils, induced myopia and visual disturbance are the effects, and a young eye with active accommodation notices them most. That, plus frequent dosing, is why compliance is poor. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How do tetracaine and proparacaine work?","choices":["They block muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","They activate alpha-1 receptors to vasoconstrict","They inhibit sodium influx into the neuron","They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the sodium channel stops the action potential, so the nerve cannot signal. It is the same mechanism as any local anaesthetic, applied to the ocular surface. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
